# Build context for apps/worker/Dockerfile.
#
# node_modules is the one that matters rather than merely being wasteful: pnpm fills a
# workspace with symlinks into the host's store, and the Dockerfile copies sources
# *after* installing. Without this, those host symlinks would land on top of the
# container's freshly installed tree and dangle. Railway builds from git, which carries
# no node_modules, so this bites the documented local build:
#
#     docker build -f apps/worker/Dockerfile -t trade-replay-worker .
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build output. The image builds its own; copying a host build in would ship whatever
# happened to be lying around.
dist
**/dist
.next
**/.next

# Nothing in the image reads these, and .git in particular is most of the context size.
.git
.github
.turbo
**/.turbo
.data
fixtures
docs
*.md
